2️⃣ LEFT JOIN — keep every row from the left table
Returns ALL rows from the left table; where there is no match in the right table, the right-side columns come back as NULL.

SELECT building_name, capacity, role, name
FROM buildings
  LEFT JOIN employees
    ON building_name = building;
Result (empty buildings appear with NULLs):

building_name	capacity	role	name
1e	24	Engineer	Becky A.
1e	24	Engineer	Dan B.
...	...	...	...
1w	32	NULL	NULL
2e	16	NULL	NULL
2w	20	Artist	Tylar S.
...	...	...	...
Practice
1. Find the list of all buildings that have employees — several valid ways:

-- Way 1: simplest
SELECT DISTINCT building FROM employees;

-- Way 2: with a count as a bonus
SELECT building, COUNT(*) AS employee_count
FROM employees
GROUP BY building;

-- Way 3: subquery of distinct buildings
SELECT building
FROM (
    SELECT DISTINCT building
    FROM employees
) AS buildings;

-- Way 4: filter with IN
SELECT building
FROM employees
WHERE building IN ('1e', '2w')
GROUP BY building;
2. Find the list of all buildings and their capacity

SELECT * FROM buildings;
3. List all buildings and the distinct employee roles in each building (including empty buildings) — the LEFT JOIN keeps empty buildings:

SELECT DISTINCT building_name, role
FROM buildings
  LEFT JOIN employees
    ON building_name = building;
Result:

building_name	role
1e	Engineer
1e	Manager
1w	NULL
2e	NULL
2w	Artist
2w	Manager
3️⃣ RIGHT JOIN — keep every row from the right table
Same as LEFT JOIN but mirrored: ALL rows from the right table are kept; unmatched left columns become NULL.

SELECT building_name, capacity, name
FROM employees
  RIGHT JOIN buildings
    ON building_name = building;
💡 A RIGHT JOIN B ≡ B LEFT JOIN A — same rows, opposite order. In practice most people write everything as LEFT JOIN and just put the "keep everything" table first.

4️⃣ FULL JOIN — keep everything from both sides
All rows from both tables; missing sides are filled with NULL.

SELECT building_name, capacity, name
FROM buildings
  FULL JOIN employees
    ON building_name = building;
MySQL does not support FULL JOIN — emulate it with LEFT JOIN ... UNION RIGHT JOIN.
5️⃣ IS NULL / IS NOT NULL — finding the unmatched rows
After an OUTER JOIN, unmatched rows show NULL — filtering on that NULL is how you find "the ones missing a match."

⚠️ Never write = NULL — always IS NULL / IS NOT NULL. NULL is "unknown", so it never equals anything.

Practice
1. Find the name and role of all employees who have not been assigned to a building

SELECT name, role FROM employees
WHERE building IS NULL;
Equivalent with a LEFT JOIN + IS NULL (find employees whose building name doesn't exist in buildings):

SELECT E.name, E.role
FROM Employees AS E
  LEFT JOIN Buildings AS B
    ON E.building = B.building_name
WHERE B.building_name IS NULL;
2. Find the names of the buildings that hold no employees

SELECT DISTINCT building_name
FROM buildings
  LEFT JOIN employees
    ON building_name = building
WHERE role IS NULL;
Result:

building_name
1w
2e
6️⃣ Anti Joins — rows with NO match on the other side
An anti join is an OUTER JOIN + WHERE <other side> IS NULL. It returns rows from one table that have no match in the other.

 LEFT ANTI JOIN                 RIGHT ANTI JOIN
 ┌────────────────┐             ┌────────────────┐
 │ A rows with    │             │ B rows with    │
 │ NO match in B  │             │ NO match in A  │
 └────────────────┘             └────────────────┘
LEFT ANTI JOIN — rows from the left with no match on the right:

SELECT *
FROM customers AS c
  LEFT JOIN orders AS o
    ON c.id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL;
-- = customers who never placed an order
RIGHT ANTI JOIN — rows from the right with no match on the left:

SELECT *
FROM customers AS c
  RIGHT JOIN orders AS o
    ON c.id = o.customer_id
WHERE c.id IS NULL;
-- = orders with no matching customer
Same thing rewritten as a LEFT JOIN (swap the tables instead of using RIGHT):

SELECT *
FROM orders AS o
  LEFT JOIN customers AS c
    ON c.id = o.customer_id
WHERE c.id IS NULL;
FULL ANTI JOIN — rows that don't match in either table (FULL JOIN + both sides NULL):

SELECT *
FROM orders AS o
  FULL JOIN customers AS c
    ON c.id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL
   OR c.id IS NULL;
7️⃣ CROSS JOIN — every combination (Cartesian product)
Combines every row from the left with every row from the right — no ON condition needed.

SELECT *
FROM customers
  CROSS JOIN orders;
Result size = rows(left) × rows(right).
Useful for generating all possible combinations (e.g. every product × every month).
⚠️ Dangerous on big tables: 1,000 × 1,000 rows = 1,000,000 results.
